egin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.3}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.7}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, the marginal mass function for $X$ is given by the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |" 13194139536202 true] [17592186161806 1100 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, find $P(X = 0)$.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |" 13194139536202 false] [17592186161806 1100 "Given the joint probability distribution $f(x,y)$ below for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ find $P(X = 0).$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ |" 13194139536202 true] [17592186161806 1145 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = x) &= f_{X}(x) = \\sum _{y}f(x, y) \\\\\nP(Y = y) &= f_{Y}(y) = \\sum _{x}f(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{12}}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{12}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = 0) &= f_{X}(0) \\\\\n&= f(0, 0) + f(0, 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6} + \\frac{5}{12} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536202 false] [17592186161806 1145 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{7}{12}}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $\\dfrac{5}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{7}{12}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, using the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=0) & ={f}_{X}(0) \\\\  & =f(0,0)+f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536202 true] [17592186161807 1100 "Given the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$, calculate $P(X \\geq 2)$.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |" 13194139536202 false] [17592186161807 1100 "| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n\nGiven the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ above for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate $P(X \\geq 2).$" 13194139536202 true] [17592186161807 1145 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X = x) &= f_{X}(x) = \\sum _{y}f(x, y) \\\\\nP(Y = y) &= f_{Y}(y) = \\sum _{x}f(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y$, respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals. Let's add these row and column totals to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.5}$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.35$ | $0.65$ | $1$ |\n\nThe marginal mass function for $X$ is given in the following table:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.25$ |\n\nFinally, using the table of values of the marginal mass function for $X$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X \\ge 2) &= P(X ∈ {2, 3}) \\\\\n&= P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) \\\\\n&= 0.5 + 0.25 \\\\\n&= 0.75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536202 false] [17592186161807 1145 "If the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have joint distribution with joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & ={f}_{X}(x)={\\sum }_{y}f(x,y) \\\\ P(Y=y) & ={f}_{Y}(y)={\\sum }_{x}f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nIn this case, the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ correspond